// extdeps/docker/docker.dag -- "What is Docker?" (the container-runtime surface
// we consume: images, mounts).
//
// Spec: https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage/ (mounts) and
//       https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/image/ (images)
// Version: Docker Engine 24.x+
//
// Facts about Docker's data model, not how we invoke it. CLI encodings
// ("-v host:ctr:ro", "--mount type=bind,...", "256m" size strings) are
// boundary syntax: parsed into these facts at ingress, re-encoded at
// egress, never stored (units discipline -- quantities are bare standard
// units; product/compute_fabric.dag models the hardware the runtime sits on).
//
// Consumers:
//   gunb-ai/ctrl plans/models/session_contract -- session bind mounts

module extdeps.docker

import std.types { FilePath, NonEmptyStr, ContentHash }

// -- Images ----------------------------------------------------------

type DockerImage {
  repository: NonEmptyStr   // e.g. "ctrl-session"
  tag: NonEmptyStr          // e.g. "latest"
  digest: ContentHash?           // Some("sha256:...") when pinned; None when by-tag only
}

// -- Mounts ----------------------------------------------------------
// Docker's three mount kinds. A bind mount maps a host path into the
// container; a volume mount attaches a daemon-managed named volume; a
// tmpfs mount is container-private memory-backed scratch.
//
// Coproduct dissolution classification (DESIGN.md §2 decompress->map->reduce):
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Docker's `--mount type=` surface is a closed,
// daemon-defined taxonomy of exactly these three kinds; the variants are
// the irreducible distinction at the docker-CLI boundary, with genuinely
// distinct payloads (a host path / a named volume / a memory size).
// Ledger (Patterns 1–5): (1) fact-placement rejected — all three flow to
// the same consumer (the container runtime), no per-consumer split.
// (2) variant-is-data rejected — not one shape with a label; payloads
// differ structurally (host_path vs volume name vs size_bytes).
// (3) no std algebraic source — these are runtime config, not ops over a
// std type. (4) not dimensional — the kinds do not factor into orthogonal
// axes; readonly is already an explicit field, not a hidden dimension.
// (5) not a parameterized family — no separately-declared set is being
// enumerated. The closed docker mount-kind set is the source.
type DockerMount
  = BindMount { host_path: FilePath, container_path: FilePath, readonly: Bool }
  | VolumeMount { volume: NonEmptyStr, container_path: FilePath, readonly: Bool }
  | TmpfsMount { container_path: FilePath, size_bytes: Int }
